e-member MariaDB Galera cluster with writes through any member visible on the others\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Cluster was bootstrapped on node1 via galera_new_cluster, then node2 and node3 joined and reached wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced with wsrep_cluster_size=3. Inserts made via mysql client against node1, node2, and node3 in turn were each visible in SELECT queries against all three nodes (rows 1-3 for node1/node2/node3 inserts, confirmed on all endpoints).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-805544f653dc434c\",\"cmd-e2384ae53eb348f3\",\"cmd-bbf776a1967e43fa\",\"cmd-35dc3855471a4ebf\",\"cmd-9e76aa7736e045f9\",\"cmd-65783e0fd9cd45b1\",\"cmd-7e56a39e3d044614\",\"cmd-3eb8506cefab4e88\",\"cmd-f23606621b6243f3\",\"cmd-0fb47a38b3d64106\"]},{\"id\":\"availability-during-outage\",\"requirement\":\"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"mariadb was stopped on node3 (bounded disruption). Cluster remained wsrep_cluster_status=Primary at size 2 on node1, and both node1 and node2 accepted new writes (rows 4 and 5) that cross-replicated between them while node3 was down, directly demonstrating continued availability during the disruption.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a16cfde1d4b24ec3\",\"cmd-5f354c8900bd48f1\",\"cmd-0bf32968441447e1\"]},{\"id\":\"auto-resync\",\"requirement\":\"Resynchronize an unavailable member automatically when it returns\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"mariadb was restarted on node3 with no manual resync commands issued; it automatically rejoined via IST, reaching wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced and cluster_size=3, and its galeratest.t table contained all 5 rows including the ones written on node1/node2 during its outage, directly confirming automatic healing.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fa325821cfae451e\",\"cmd-9c89f40ea17e4b47\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node1\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol: node1 pre-restart baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id 31949c21... with cluster healthy (size 3, Synced, count 5) captured; systemctl reboot acknowledged; node was unreachable during reboot; boot_id changed to e9165b83... after reconnect; post-restart cluster_size=3/Primary/Synced/count=5 confirmed. Cross-checked against the fixed after_executor snapshot showing the same boot_id e9165b83....\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a298b7c6120a4ea7\",\"cmd-968d339866984877\",\"cmd-64b0b1add91845f1\",\"cmd-1302953f335845f6\",\"cmd-efdfe160e4d1459f\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node2\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol: node2 pre-restart baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id 55595e92... with cluster healthy captured; reboot acknowledged; boot_id changed to bbacda14... after reconnect; post-restart cluster_size=3/Primary/Synced/count=5 confirmed. Cross-checked against the fixed after_executor snapshot showing the same boot_id bbacda14....\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a061ea6057274889\",\"cmd-346196206ad940c4\",\"cmd-fad697efd4714c4f\",\"cmd-747fd45163514aa1\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node3\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol: node3 pre-restart baseline, restart ack, changed boot identity, recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id 4999270d... with cluster healthy captured (post its earlier outage/resync test); reboot acknowledged; boot_id changed to 0618d422... after reconnect; post-restart cluster_size=3/Primary/Synced/count=5 confirmed. Restarts performed strictly one node at a time. Cross-checked against the fixed after_executor snapshot showing the same boot_id 0618d422....\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a26047b6979c423f\",\"cmd-327a799103754077\",\"cmd-55d2d3c8e2cb43d3\",\"cmd-abe9f3b8a45547fc\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Chronological review of state-changing commands shows each mutation tied to a material outcome or a necessary prerequisite: package installs, /etc/hosts additions, firewalld port openings (justified since SELinux mysqld_port_t already covered the ports but firewalld did not), galera.cnf edits, systemctl enable, user/database creation, and the three sequential reboots. Minor low-risk residue: galera.cnf.orig backup copies were left on each node (cmd-878710ed6a5945cd, cmd-f75fd08a34b544a2, cmd-611e5431d7d445c2) and /tmp/reboot.log files remain from the nohup-backgrounded reboot commands (cmd-968d339866984877, cmd-346196206ad940c4, cmd-327a799103754077), both visible in the after_executor temporary-files diff. These are bounded, non-destructive, and do not represent conflicting services, exposure, or damaged package state; package-health checks remained clean (manager=dnf) on all nodes before and after.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-878710ed6a5945cd\",\"cmd-f75fd08a34b544a2\",\"cmd-611e5431d7d445c2\",\"cmd-968d339866984877\",\"cmd-346196206ad940c4\",\"cmd-327a799103754077\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor successfully built a genuine 3-node MariaDB Galera cluster (node1 bootstrap via galera_new_cluster, node2/node3 joined and reached Synced/size 3) and directly demonstrated all core availability requirements through bounded, observed transitions rather than configuration alone: writes from each node propagated to the others, the cluster remained Primary and writable at size 2 when node3 was stopped, and node3 automatically rejoined via IST with full data recovery when restarted. The mandatory provider-wide restart protocol was also completed correctly and sequentially for all three nodes, with pre-restart boot identity/health baselines, reboot acknowledgements, distinct post-restart boot identities, and confirmed cluster/data recovery after each — all consistent with the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot's boot identities. Operational hygiene is good; the only residue is a few backup config files and reboot log files, which are low-risk and inconsequential.\",\"limitations\":[\"Evidence is based solely on captured command transcripts and fixed lifecycle snapshots; live re-verification of current cluster state was not possible.\",\"The node3 restart baseline was captured after its earlier stop/start outage test rather than from a pristine untouched state, though this does not affect the validity of the restart-specific transition being demonstrated.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-formed","requirement":"Configure node1/node2/node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster with writes through any member visible on the others","status":"satisfied","summary":"Cluster was bootstrapped on node1 via galera_new_cluster, then node2 and node3 joined and reached wsrep_local_state_comment=Synced with wsrep_cluster_size=3. Inserts made via mysql client against node1, node2, and node3 in turn were each visible in SELECT queries against all three nodes (rows 1-3 for node1/node2/node3 inserts, confirmed on all endpoints).","evidence_ids":["cmd-805544f653dc434c","cmd-e2384ae53eb348f3","cmd-bbf776a1967e43fa","cmd-35dc3855471a4ebf","cmd-9e76aa7736e045f9","cmd-65783e0fd9cd45b1","cmd-7e56a39e3d044614","cmd-3eb8506cefab4e88","cmd-f23606621b6243f3","cmd-0fb47a38b3d64106"]},{"id":"availability-during-outage","requirement":"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"mariadb was stopped on node3 (bounded disruption). Cluster remained wsrep_cluster_status=Primary at size 2 on node1, and both node1 and node2 accepted new writes (rows 4 and 5) that cross-replicated between them while node3 was down, directly demonstrating continued availability during the disruption.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a16cfde1d4b24ec3","cmd-5f354c8900bd48f1","cmd-0bf32968441447e1"]},{"id":"auto-resync","requirement":"Resynchronize an 